 on this  panel I'm going to start off the debate  with a question are humans incapable of  weighing up risk rationally so Stephen  can we start with you  it's a strange question humans compared  to who compared to God compared to some  advanced race of space aliens obviously  some humans must be capable of weighing  up risks or we have no benchmark that  would allow us to judge whether anyone  is being rational or irrational and  obviously when it comes to everyday  risks we're all capable of some rational  judgment because we the world is a  dangerous place and and most of us stay  alive the annual death rate from  accidents is less than 1/100 of 1% so  that means most of the time people  aren't driving into incoming traffic  people don't take a shortcut from the  top floor of a tall building by jumping  over the balcony they take stairs  they keep kids out of danger and and  contrary to the advice of a certain  world leader people don't drink bleach  to ward off infectious disease that's  the work of our fellow panelists Danny  Kahneman shows that we don't appreciate  much smaller risks that can only be  ascertained through large data sets that  give us our actuarial probabilities an  obvious example is that people are far  too afraid plane travel compared to  automobile travel because of the  availability of images and anecdotes of  plane disasters after 9/11 for example  that are publicly an extra 1,500 extra  deaths in the United States when people  stayed off planes which they  irrationally thought were dangerous and  drove instead which was far more  hazardous so really the question should  be reframed as went for the judgments of  some humans license restrictions on the  choices of other humans and this raises  some additional questions the issues  that we posed at the beginning were  couched in terms of the we but the  question is who is the we and practice  we're talking about government coercion  over individual choices so the question  is when are those license the answers  got to be sometimes but in generally  general not all actions have risks  because you can't just do one thing we  wanted perfect safety we would stay in  bed all day and  that isn't perfectly safe and there's no  there can't be a rational answer to what  is the correct level of risk among a set  of alternatives each one of which poses  some risk although there can be  irrational answers how to keep those  choices consistent and since there is no  no such thing as we they're only some  humans and Evos in government exerting  power over other humans the question is  how can we ensure that those wielding  power do so in in justifiable ways given  that they themselves are subject to the  same irrationality since everyone else  the obvious example being responses to  terrorism where in order to keep their  populations safe the leaders of our  governments started to catastrophic wars  set up a whole bureaucracy that with a  threat to civil liberties order for  homeland security to protect people  against their risk namely terrorism  which is a tiny fraction of the risks  that we incur from other sources finally  since these questions are being raised  during the covert pandemic it's  essential to make a key distinction that  we're not talking about risks that  individuals incur such as whether  someone who has cancer chooses to take  some elective but potentially dangerous  therapy because we're talking about  diseases that are infectious where your  actions are do not simply involve risk  that you assume yourself but risks that  you impose on other people and that  completely reframes the debate it's more  similar to do we allow someone to spray  machine-gun fire in a crowded place  their liberty is kind of beside the  point when it comes to the cost that  they're imposing on others and in most  of the debates that were having now that  is what is at issue not people's  willingness to incur whisks themselves  show me your take on that question so  you're picking up very much from from  where Stephen left gamblers trace it  actuaries counter to insurance brokers  to monetize it but but I generally form  my views on risk  from human rights perspective I attempt  to value all lives equally rather than  sum over others but that is complex in  practice and it requires the  consideration of civil and political  rights as well as social economic and  cultural ones now given as we've heard  that there's no such thing as a  risk-free life or society the balancing  of risks can't simply be done on the  basis of statistics or for example  numbers of deaths from a particular  activity or course of action there are  other perhaps less quantifiable risks  for example to an individual's life or  quality of life from for example banning  alcohol or mountaineering or or even  from using torture in the infamous  ticking bomb scenario conversely to  completely ignore the good of the many  and just always make individual  judgments would effectively mean the end  of society and it would be intolerably  unjust to the vulnerable and not least  again as Steven said in the case of the  spread of a deadly and highly infectious  pandemic so a human rights approach  identifies which individual rights are  absolutes and which are little more  qualified in and in the case of that  latter  and larger group of liberties  interferences with the individual  freedom may be justified for the  protection of others when proportionate  and crucially when applied in a  non-discriminatory manner now leaving  people to take certain risks with their  own light is a good liberal principle  for any democratic society but not when  they are also risking the lives of  others hence we don't ban alcohol but we  regulate its strength and its sale and  we forgive people driving under its  influence that's why I think some of the  more libertarian complains about the  covert 19 lock down have seemed so  selfish and out of kilter with a lot of  the general public mood if not all of it  Thank You Daniel your take this subject  is very much any or we love well  the question are humans incapable of  weighing risk rationally is ambiguous  does it refer to individual humans or  with humanity in other interpretation  the short answer to the question is no  individuals and societies are certainly  capable of rational reasonable action I  prefer to avoid the term rationality  because it's a technical term and  certainly people are not able to attack  Russian but they are capable of acting  reasonably now I prefer to focus on  societies and the ability of societies  throughout rationally society that are  guided by good science can deal  appropriately with threats that are  familiar and immediate like hurricanes  however some threats Pro the challenge  to the human mind and there are two that  are highly prominent these days threats  that draw experiencing me and with the  lab are completely incomprehensible so  27 percent increase the daily increase  in the rate of a pandemic means doubling  every three days and it means that if 30  days  it grows by a factor of 1,000 the human  mind is completely incapable of dealing  with it with exponential growth so  threats of that kind risks of that kind  will we'll need some other treatment  than human intuition of human unaided  reason to continue watching this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit a IDOT TV for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas  you

